\date{2026-01-28T21:19:54Z}
\tag{discrete}
\taxon{Example}

\import{discrete-macros}

\p{Show that #{P \to Q \equiv \neg P \lor Q}.}

\p{One way we could do this is by making a truth table:
##{
  \begin{array}{ccccc}
      P & Q & P \to Q & \neg P & \neg P \lor Q \\ \hline
      \True & \True & \True & \False & \True \\
      \True & \False & \False & \False & \False \\
      \False & \True & \True & \True & \True \\
      \False & \False & \True & \True & \True \\
  \end{array}
}
  Notice that the columns for #{P \implies Q} and #{\neg P \lor Q} are
  exactly the same, which means that these two propositions always
  have the same truth value.  Hence they are logically equivalent.}

\p{We could also use Disco to check this automatically, like so:
\pre\verb<<<|
Disco> :test forall p: Bool, q:Bool. (p -> q) == (not p \/ q)
  - Certainly true: ∀p, q. (p -> q) == (not p \/ q)
    No counterexamples exist; all possible values were checked.
<<<
}
